#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407816} {:answer/id #uuid "6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407821} {:answer/id #uuid "84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407823} {:answer/id #uuid "a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" :question/math-academy-id "q-183509" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ $y<y+1⇒{y}^{-1}\\in Q.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$y<y+1$\" is ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{-1}\\in Q$\" is ${Z}^{-}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{Z}^{-}}\\cup {Z}^{-}=∅\\cup {Z}^{-}={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\notin Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407813} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every prime number $y$ is rational." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407814} {:answer/id #uuid "945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d1dbbf-3579-5af2-80d8-490559725897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $y\\in Q,$ $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407830} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5dfd88-d11e-5abc-a681-95fc1039e544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is not prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} :answer-field/id #uuid "afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016" :question/math-academy-id "q-183517" :question/problem "$\\,$ $K(y):$ $y$ is prime\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $y\\in Q$ **such that** $y$ is prime.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407820} {:answer/id #uuid "7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407822} {:answer/id #uuid "b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" :question/math-academy-id "q-183552" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}\\in N$ and $n+5>1$ \n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{2}\\in N$\" is the set $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+5>1$\" is $N.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=N\\cap N=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407815} {:answer/id #uuid "339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407817} {:answer/id #uuid "3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407818} {:answer/id #uuid "424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407819} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216465" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(m):$ $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is ${2k:k\\in Z}.$\n- $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58e92df-9945-5743-8088-4250afd4e285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "c523af15-9d38-59e1-a24b-c6e9af255c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "c64e638f-c63c-542f-96c1-65992079bca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ba58d-5e8a-5213-afcf-34f6a5938516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf6d984-9d9e-5c92-a6cf-995e484dfb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58e92df-9945-5743-8088-4250afd4e285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} :answer-field/id #uuid "589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
